# Biomedical Equipment Error Log Triage

INF1103 Team Project (Phase 2, object-oriented) by team **JSON Derulo**
(INF1103-Team-2).

Hospital machines produce many error logs. This app reads them, asks an AI to
assess each one (severity, patient-safety risk, likely cause), applies our
business rules, alerts engineers on Telegram, and stores everything in a JSON
file. It follows the fixed architecture
`io_manager → ai_manager → logic_manager → data_manager`, with one class per
file.

You can use it in three ways:

| Way | Command | Best for |
|---|---|---|
| Command-line menu | `python main.py` | Everyday use, scripts, Docker |
| Desktop window (GUI) | `python main.py --gui` | Clicking instead of typing; needs a screen |
| Docker | `docker run ...` (see below) | Same setup on every laptop; CLI and tests only |

The new feature (HTML / Markdown report) works in all three. The GUI
(advanced feature) runs on your own computer only, not inside Docker.

## 1. Before you start

### 1.1 Open a terminal in the project folder

In File Explorer, open the `Project` folder, click the address bar, type
`powershell`, and press Enter. This opens PowerShell in that folder without
needing to use a specific computer's path. Alternatively, use `Set-Location`
with the path to the `Project` folder on your own computer:

```powershell
Set-Location "{path\to\your}\Project"
```

The current folder should contain `Dockerfile`, `requirements.txt`, and
`main.py`. All commands in this README assume PowerShell and that folder.

### 1.2 Prepare the `.env` file

Create or edit `.env` in the project folder. Add your own API credentials and
settings, or request the team's `.env` file from the Group 2 leader through a
private channel. Do not post credentials in the README, source code, or a
public chat, and do not commit `.env`.

```dotenv
AI_API_KEY=your_api_key
AI_BASE_URL=https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
AI_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
DATA_FILE=data/processed_records.json
```

`AI_BASE_URL` and `AI_MODEL` should match the AI provider and model for your
key. Telegram settings are optional; when they are empty, messages are shown
as a "DRY RUN" instead of being sent. Without an AI key the app still runs,
but every record goes to human review.

`DATA_FILE=data/processed_records.json` makes the local CLI and the GUI use
the **same records as Docker** (Docker saves to the same `data` folder; see
section 4). Docker still works with this line, because the `-e DATA_FILE=...`
in the `docker run` command takes priority over the value in `.env`.

The Docker image does not contain `.env`; `docker run --env-file .env` passes
the values into the container at runtime.

### 1.3 Create the data, log and report folders

```powershell
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force .\data, .\logs, .\reports
```

| Folder | What goes there |
|---|---|
| `data` | `processed_records.json`, all processed records |
| `logs` | Machine log files you want to ingest, plus the app's own `app.log` |
| `reports` | HTML / Markdown reports created by menu option 7 or the GUI |

## 2. Run on your own computer (CLI and GUI)

### 2.1 One-time setup

1. Install **Python 3.12**. On Windows, tick **"tcl/tk and IDLE"** in the
   installer; the GUI needs it.
2. Install the libraries:

```powershell
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

If `python` or `pip` is not found, use `py` / `py -m pip` (Windows) or
`python3` / `pip3` (macOS and Linux).

### 2.2 Command-line menu

```powershell
python main.py
```

| Option | What it does |
|---|---|
| 1. Enter a single error log | Type one log; the AI assesses it and the rules triage it |
| 2. Ingest a log file | Read a whole file (format below), e.g. `logs\errors.txt` |
| 3. View all stored records | Table sorted by priority; optionally show the top record in full |
| 4. Query stored records | Immediate escalations, safety risks, human review, or by machine ID |
| 5. Review pending records | Approve the AI severity or enter your own |
| 6. Fleet summary report | Totals, routes and machines with repeat faults, on screen |
| 7. Generate HTML / Markdown report | **New feature**: saves a report file in `reports\` |
| 8. Exit | Save and close |

Log file format, one error per line:

```text
timestamp | machine_id | error_code | machine_type | error message
2026-09-16 21:30:05 | CT-114 | E102 | CT scanner | Coolant temperature exceeded 90C
```

Lines that do not match are skipped and listed on screen. If no line is
valid, the app shows "Invalid log format. Upload aborted." (Business Rule 4).

### 2.3 Desktop window (GUI)

```powershell
python main.py --gui
```

| Button / area | What it does |
|---|---|
| + Add error log | Form with the same checks as the CLI; sends the log to the AI |
| Import log file | Pick a log file; skipped lines appear in the Activity panel |
| Review pending (n) | One dialog per record: approve the AI, enter your own, skip or stop |
| Generate report | Create an HTML or Markdown report and open it in your browser |
| Show / Machine ID | Filter the table or search for one machine |
| Record details | Everything about the selected record |
| Activity | Live messages: AI progress, Telegram results, errors |

AI and Telegram calls run in the background, so the window does not freeze.
Urgent errors also pop up as an alert.

**Always start the GUI (and the CLI) from the project folder.** The
`data`, `logs` and `reports` paths are relative to the folder you start in.
If you start from somewhere else, the app will look for a different (empty)
records file.

If you see `No module named 'tkinter'`: on Windows, re-run the Python
installer, choose **Modify**, and tick **tcl/tk and IDLE**. On macOS with
Homebrew, run `brew install python-tk`. On Linux, run
`sudo apt install python3-tk`.

### 2.4 Offline tests

```powershell
python test_logic.py
```

21 tests check the business rules, storage, Telegram messages, the pipeline,
the bug fixes, the report and the GUI notifier. AI and Telegram are mocked, so
no credentials or internet are needed. The GUI test is skipped automatically
when Tkinter is not installed.

## 3. Run in Docker (CLI and tests)

### 3.1 Start Docker Desktop

Open Docker Desktop and wait until its engine is running.

### 3.2 Build the Docker image

```powershell
docker build -t inf1103-group2-errorlogging .
```

This builds an image named `inf1103-group2-errorlogging` using the
`Dockerfile` in the current folder. Rebuild it after changing application code
or the Dockerfile.

### 3.3 Run the interactive app with mounted folders

```powershell
docker run --rm -it `
  --mount "type=bind,source=$((Get-Location).Path)\data,target=/data" `
  --mount "type=bind,source=$((Get-Location).Path)\logs,target=/app/logs" `
  --mount "type=bind,source=$((Get-Location).Path)\reports,target=/app/reports" `
  --env-file .env `
  -e DATA_FILE=/data/processed_records.json `
  inf1103-group2-errorlogging
```

The `data` mount maps `Project\data` on your computer to `/data` in the
container. Records are saved to `Project\data\processed_records.json` and
remain there after the container exits. The `DATA_FILE` setting tells
`RecordRepository` (in `data_manager_record_repository.py`) to use that
mounted location.

The `logs` mount maps `Project\logs` to `/app/logs`. The app writes
`app.log` there; you can also put machine log files in this folder and select
them for ingestion by entering a path such as `/app/logs/errors.txt` when
prompted. This mount is writable so the app can create its diagnostic log.
If you do not need file ingestion, you can leave `logs` empty; the app still
runs normally.

The `reports` mount maps `Project\reports` to `/app/reports`, so reports
from menu option 7 are kept on your computer. Without this mount they would
be deleted together with the container.

`--rm` removes the container when it exits, while the mounted data, log and
report files stay on your computer. Select **Exit** in the app menu to stop it.

The GUI cannot run in Docker (a container has no screen). If you run
`python main.py --gui` inside the container, the app prints a short message
and stops.

### 3.4 Run the offline tests in Docker

After building the image, run:

```powershell
docker run --rm inf1103-group2-errorlogging python test_logic.py
```

Expected result: `All 21 tests passed.` To rebuild before testing after code
changes:

```powershell
docker build -t inf1103-group2-errorlogging .
docker run --rm inf1103-group2-errorlogging python test_logic.py
```

## 4. How the CLI, GUI and Docker share data

| Run with | Records file used |
|---|---|
| Local CLI / GUI with `DATA_FILE=data/processed_records.json` in `.env` | `Project\data\processed_records.json` |
| Docker with the `data` mount (section 3.3) | `Project\data\processed_records.json` (seen as `/data/...` inside) |

Both rows point at the same file, so records added in the GUI are visible in
the Docker CLI and the other way round. Do not run both at the same time;
they would overwrite each other's saves.

## 5. Business rules

| Rule | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| 1. Immediate alert | High severity + patient-safety risk + confident AI → Telegram straight away |
| 2. Non-urgent batching | Other alerts are queued and sent in one report after 07:00 |
| 3. Repeat suppression | Same machine + same error code within 10 minutes → one notification |
| 4. Invalid file | File without valid lines → "Invalid log format. Upload aborted." |
| 5. Confidence threshold | AI confidence **below 70%** → pending human review, no automatic Telegram alert |

## 6. Project structure

One class per file, named `<manager>_<class>.py`.

| Manager | Files (class) |
|---|---|
| main | `main.py` (builds the objects, no class), `main_triage_app.py` (CLI menu), `main_gui_app.py` (GUI controller), `main_triage_pipeline.py` (shared processing) |
| io_manager | `io_manager_console_ui.py`, `io_manager_output_view.py` (interface), `io_manager_log_file_reader.py`, `io_manager_notifier.py` (interface), `io_manager_console_notifier.py`, `io_manager_telegram_notifier.py` |
| io_manager: report | `io_manager_report_manager.py`, `io_manager_report_formatter.py` (abstract), `io_manager_html_report_formatter.py`, `io_manager_markdown_report_formatter.py` |
| io_manager: GUI | `io_manager_gui_window.py`, `io_manager_gui_notifier.py` |
| ai_manager | `ai_manager_ai_client.py` |
| logic_manager | `logic_manager_error_log.py`, `logic_manager_decision.py`, `logic_manager_assessment.py` (abstract), `logic_manager_ai_assessment.py`, `logic_manager_human_assessment.py`, `logic_manager_triage_engine.py`, `logic_manager_alert_scheduler.py`, `logic_manager_fleet_analyzer.py` |
| data_manager | `data_manager_record_repository.py` |
| Tests | `test_logic.py` |
| UML | `uml/0_overview.png` to `uml/7_gui_feature.png`, with PlantUML sources (`.puml`) and the shared `style.iuml` |
| Docs | `docs/screenshots/`, `docs/sample_report.html`, `docs/sample_report.md` |

## 7. Troubleshooting

| Problem | Fix |
|---|---|
| `No module named 'tkinter'` | See the end of section 2.3 |
| `No module named 'dotenv'` or `'requests'` | `pip install -r requirements.txt` |
| `python` not found | Use `py` (Windows) or `python3` (macOS / Linux) |
| Records seem to have disappeared | You started the app from another folder, or `.env` has no `DATA_FILE` line; see sections 1.2 and 2.3 |
| Docker: cannot connect to the engine | Open Docker Desktop and wait until the engine is running |
| Docker: report not on my computer | Add the `reports` mount from section 3.3 |
| `! Could not save record ...` | The `data` folder is missing; run the `New-Item` command in section 1.3 |
| Every record goes to human review | `AI_API_KEY` is missing or wrong in `.env` |
| Telegram shows "DRY RUN" | `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` / `TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID` are empty in `.env` |
